[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: StrictDoc tooling for this repository

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-FINGERPRINT-IN-NODE
TITLE: Store the parent contract fingerprint in the node
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A second consumer needs per-edge state that a node field cannot carry, such as
a reviewer identity or a waiver with its own expiry.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
A node records the contract hash of each parent it depends on, in a PARENT_FP
field on the node itself, rather than in a sidecar file keyed by node identity.
Fingerprints point strictly downward: DECISION to MECHANISM to SLICE.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Accepting a change becomes a reviewable git diff, attributable to a commit and
revertable. A sidecar makes sign-off invisible and needs a key-matching scheme.
The downward-only rule means a garbage-collectable node has no inbound
fingerprint, so deleting it can never strand one.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY
TITLE: Implementability is a property of a closure, not of the project
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A release needs a single project-wide readiness answer, which no per-slice
query can supply.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
A slice is implementable when its own transitive contract-dependency closure is
settled and carries no suspect edges. There is no global readiness gate. The
count of open decisions in that closure is the slice's degrees of freedom, and
handoff is that count reaching zero.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
A global fail-closed gate is waterfall by construction: nothing is implementable
until everything is designed. A closure-local predicate lets a mature slice ship
while the rest of the graph stays deliberately fuzzy.
<<<

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-DEFER-LAYER
TITLE: Omit the LAYER node type until a corpus with layers arrives
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The first document whose mechanisms sit in distinct architectural layers, at
which point the cast tables' locus column becomes the LAYER assignment.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The grammar ships without LAYER. Horizontal position is therefore unmodelled;
only the vertical axis, a SLICE and the mechanisms it Crosses, is expressible.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
No content in this document sits in a layer. Adding LAYER now would make every
MECHANISM carry a field with one possible value.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
This is a real gap, not a cost-free deferral: half the horizontal-and-vertical
tracking the operator asked for is absent until it is closed.
<<<

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-CHECK
TITLE: Suspect-link detection over the JSON export
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Hash each node's contract-bearing fields from the JSON export. A node is suspect
when a parent it records a fingerprint for no longer hashes to the recorded
value, or when that parent has been deleted. Excludes RATIONALE and NOTES.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
StrictDoc has no suspect-link feature. It is on the upstream backlog as
SDOC-SRS-117 and described unbuilt in their DO-178 notes.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-FINGERPRINT-IN-NODE:0000000
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-FINGERPRINT-IN-NODE
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-CYCLE-CHECK
TITLE: Cycle detection over the JSON export
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Detect relation cycles in the exported graph independently of StrictDoc.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
StrictDoc's own cycle detection queries the graph without specifying an edge
role, and the graph database returns only the bucket of role-less edges. Every
relation in this grammar carries a role, so no cycle in this corpus is detected;
the failure surfaces as a recursion-depth crash during render.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY:0000000
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY
  ROLE: Governed_By

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-READY-QUERY
TITLE: Ready-slice query over the contract closure
DEPTH: needs-design
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Walk a slice's contract-dependency closure and report whether every mechanism in
it is interface-settled or better, every decision accepted, and no edge suspect.
Report the blocking frontier and the open-decision count otherwise. The same
traversal produces the bounded prompt handed to an implementing model.
<<<
PARENT_FP: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY:0000000
NOTES: >>>
A real gap surfaced implementing SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE: its closure crosses
MECH-SEMBLE-EXTRA-GRAMMARS and MECH-SEMBLE-PATH-MAPPINGS, both carrying
PARENT_FP: DEC-ARCH-COLOCATION, and that decision's STATUS is still open. By
this mechanism's own definition the slice was not implementable, yet the
operator directed implementation anyway and it shipped clean. Until this query
exists, nothing catches that gap except a human (or a model) noticing --
exactly the case this mechanism is meant to replace.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: DEC-LOCAL-IMPLEMENTABILITY
  ROLE: Governed_By

[SPIKE]
UID: SPIKE-SGRA-PARSE
TITLE: Make the tree-sitter grammar parse standalone .sgra files
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: run
RETIRES_ON: >>>
A three-rule patch is applied and the two .sgra files parse with no regression
across the sdoc corpus.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The upstream tree-sitter grammar parses all sdoc documents but fails every
standalone .sgra grammar file, because its root rule requires a DOCUMENT header.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Run. The DOCUMENT-header fix alone was not sufficient: our own grammar.sgra
also uses REVERSE_ROLE on every non-File Parent relation, and upstream's
grammar_relation_parent/child rules had no field for it at all, so it still
errored after the root-rule fix. The applied patch is therefore three rule
changes across two files (source_file's root choice, a new standalone_grammar
rule, and REVERSE_ROLE added to both relation rules) rather than one.

Measured against this repo's own corpus (6 .sdoc + grammar.sgra): 6/7 clean
before the patch, 7/7 after. Measured against strictdoc-project/strictdoc's
own docs/ (15 files, pinned rev 215cda2e): 5/15 clean both before and after —
the other 10 fail on pre-existing document-body/FREETEXT coverage gaps this
patch never targeted (out of DEC-GRAMMAR-PATCH-NOT-FORK's scope; logged as
MECH-SGRA-BODY-COVERAGE in the plan backlog). checks/strictdoc-grammar-corpus.nix
is the standing regression gate against both corpora.
<<<

[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-SDOC-IN-SEMBLE
TITLE: Index sdoc documents in semble
DEPTH: verified
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
Package a tree-sitter grammar for the sdoc format as a flake output, add it to
ai.programs.semble.grammars, and register a path mapping assigning *.sdoc to
that language in the docs index. Add a load check modelled on the existing
extra-grammars check, parsing a real sdoc sample.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The repository's flake instructions already require that a custom grammar absent
from nixpkgs be exposed as a flake package so the authenticated matrix publishes
it. Every mechanism this slice crosses is already implemented.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Implemented: overlays/generic/tree-sitter-strictdoc.nix (flake package
tree-sitter-strictdoc, flattened via pkgs.ai.generic), wired into
ai.codex.programs.semble.grammars and mcp.pathMappings in devenv.nix (patterns
*.sdoc and *.sgra, content docs). Load check:
checks/module-eval.nix:module-semble-strictdoc-grammar-load. Regression/corpus
suite: checks/strictdoc-grammar-corpus.nix (see SPIKE-SGRA-PARSE for measured
counts). update-targets and cache-hit-parity rows added; both checks pass.

Chunking verified independently, not assumed: on a 8762-byte document the AST
chunker returns 17 chunks against line chunking's 12, and the boundaries land
on node headers and on field starts, so a node larger than the 750-character
target splits into its own fields. chunk() returning a list rather than None is
the proof the parser loaded; a fallback would have been a log warning only.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-SEMBLE-EXTRA-GRAMMARS
  ROLE: Crosses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-SEMBLE-PATH-MAPPINGS
  ROLE: Crosses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SPIKE-SGRA-PARSE
  ROLE: Proven_By

[SLICE]
UID: SLICE-FP-DETECTOR
TITLE: Suspect-link detection and acceptance over the design graph
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: human
STATEMENT: >>>
Ship fp-check and fp-accept over the JSON export, plus a flake check that gates
on fp-check with a clean output directory.

fp-check hashes each node's contract-bearing fields and compares every
PARENT_FP entry against the current hash of the parent it names. It reports
three cases distinctly: never signed, where the recorded hash is the
placeholder; drifted, where a real recorded hash no longer matches; and
unbacked, where a fingerprint names a parent the node has no relation to.

fp-accept rewrites the named nodes' entries to current hashes and is the only
thing that writes them. It refuses a parent that is still moving.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The contract field selection is a prototype choice, deliberately not settled --
see MECH-FP-FIELD-TUNING. Git-flow handling is deliberately absent: nothing
bites before a branch reaches main, and that merge is the forcing function.
<<<
PARENT_FP: >>>
MECH-FP-ACCEPT-READINESS:0000000
MECH-FP-CHECK:0000000
MECH-FP-RELATION-LINT:0000000
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Implemented: dev/scripts/fp-check.py and fp-accept.py, sharing dev/scripts/
sdoc_fp.py (contract-field hashing, PARENT_FP parsing, the readiness
predicate) and sdoc_edit.py (structural PARENT_FP field editor -- finds a
node's line-range by UID and field-range by name rather than splitting on
blank lines, because a multiline field's own prose can contain blank
paragraph breaks). CI gate: checks/strictdoc-fp-check.nix, exporting the
whole graph into a fresh runCommand $TMPDIR on every build. strictdoc added
to the interactive devShell (devenv.nix) for the skill's format/export loop
and for running these scripts by hand.

fp-check's first real run over this repo's existing graph found 5 nodes
across docs/plans/beads-migration and packages/semble/.sdoc with a PARENT_FP
entry and no matching relation -- exactly MECH-FP-RELATION-LINT's predicted
defect class, not a tooling bug. Fixed as their own commit (the missing
Governed_By edge each PARENT_FP already implied; nothing else about those
nodes changed) before wiring the check into flake.nix, so the gate starts
green rather than red on day one.

Verified past the happy path: a synthetic export exercises all four
fp-check cases (signed-and-current, never-signed, drifted, unbacked) plus a
deleted-parent case in one run, and a synthetic readiness fixture confirms
fp-accept actually refuses a DECISION parent with STATUS open and a
MECHANISM parent below interface-settled rather than silently skipping the
check. fp-accept was then run for real on SLICE-FP-DETECTOR itself (this
node) and on MECH-FP-CHECK and MECH-FP-ACCEPT-READINESS, producing the
PARENT_FP entries above and below -- the multi-entry block form is this
node's own three-parent write, exercised for real rather than only in the
synthetic fixture.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-FP-CHECK
  ROLE: Crosses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-FP-ACCEPT-READINESS
  ROLE: Crosses
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: MECH-FP-RELATION-LINT
  ROLE: Crosses

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-FP-RELATION-LINT
TITLE: Every PARENT_FP entry should have a matching relation
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A node recording a fingerprint for a parent asserts a dependency on that
parent's contract, so it should also carry an edge to it. Nothing enforces
that today, and several nodes in this graph already have a fingerprint with no
corresponding relation.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Without the lint the two can disagree silently, and the edge is what the
traversal follows. A fingerprint the traversal cannot reach is invisible to both
the drift check and the render cache.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Realized as fp-check's "unbacked" case (dev/scripts/fp-check.py) rather than
a separate binary -- the check already walks every PARENT_FP entry against
the node's RELATIONS, so a second pass over the same data would just be the
first one run twice. Its first real run over this repo's own graph found 5
pre-existing violations; see SLICE-FP-DETECTOR's NOTES.
<<<
